/-!
# A nonempty regular open descends along a finite type inclusion of domains

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 07PA, `lemma-J-0-goes-down`, with J-0
expanded using Tag 07P7, `definition-J`
(blueprint: lem-exc-j0-finite-type-domain-descent).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

namespace RingHom

/-- A Noetherian domain has a nonempty regular open if it embeds as a finite type subring
of a domain having a nonempty regular open.

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 07PA, `lemma-J-0-goes-down`; Tag 07P7,
`definition-J` (blueprint: lem-exc-j0-finite-type-domain-descent).

Proof, following the seven steps of the node:
1. Choose $\mathfrak q_0\in V$. By the basic-open basis lemma choose $g\in S$ with $\mathfrak
   q_0\in D(g)\subseteq V$; in particular $g\ne0$. Put $B=S_g=S[1/g]$. This is an $R$-algebra of
   finite type: if $s_1,\ldots,s_n$ generate $S$ over $R$, their images together with $g^{-1}$
   generate $B$, since every fraction is $s/g^m$. It is a nonzero domain and $R\to B$ is
   injective: the zero-fraction criterion says that an element killed in $B$ is killed in $S$ by
   a power of $g$, which is nonzero in the domain $S$. Thus a nonzero $r\in R$ cannot be killed.
   Uses: lem-weil-pbc-spec-localization-away-basic-open, def-weil-ring-localization-away, def-
   weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra.

2. The finite-type Noetherian permanence lemma applied to $R\to B$ makes $B$ Noetherian. For any
   prime $P$ of $B$, the basic-open correspondence gives its contraction $\mathfrak q\in
   D(g)\subseteq V$. The localizations $B_P$ and $S_{\mathfrak q}$ are canonically isomorphic:
   $(s/g^m)/(t/g^n)$ maps to $sg^n/(tg^m)$, and the inverse sends $s/t$ to $(s/1)/(t/1)$. The
   denominators are legitimate because $g\notin\mathfrak q$ and $t/g^n\notin P$ precisely when
   $t\notin\mathfrak q$; cross multiplication and the zero-fraction relations make the two maps
   well-defined and inverse. By the hypothesis on $V$, every $B_P$ is regular local.
   Consequently $B$ is a regular ring by definition. Regular-local status is invariant under
   this isomorphism: it identifies the maximal ideals and their generating families, all ideals
   for Noetherianity, and prime chains for Krull dimension.
   Uses: lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra-noetherian, lem-weil-pbc-spec-localization-away-basic-
   open, def-weil-ring-localization-away, def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime, def-weil-regular-
   scheme.

3. Apply Noetherian generic freeness to the original base $R$, the finite-type $R$-algebra $B$,
   and the $B$-module $M=B$, which is generated by $1$. All hypotheses hold: $R$ is the given
   Noetherian domain and step 1 proved finite type. It gives $0\ne a\in R$ such that $T=B_a$ is
   free over $A=R_a$. The localized module $(M)_a$ here is exactly the underlying $A$-module of
   $T$: both have fractions $b/a^j$ with the same equality criterion and scalar multiplication.
   No finiteness of the free basis over $A$ is asserted or needed.
   Uses: lem-pur-noetherian-algebra-module-generic-freeness.

4. By localization of a regular ring, the ring $T=B_a$ is regular, since $B$ is regular by step
   2. It is nonzero: the image of $a$ in $B$ is nonzero by the injectivity from step 1, and the
   domain zero-fraction criterion prevents $1/1=0$ in $B_a$. Choose a basis $(e_i)_{i\in I}$ for
   the free $A$-module $T$ supplied in step 3. Its index set $I$ is nonempty, since the module
   with empty basis is zero. This checks the faithfulness issue which would fail for a zero free
   module.
   Uses: lem-weil-pbc-localization-of-regular-ring, def-weil-ring-localization-away, def-weil-
   pbc-free-module.

5. Here is the elementary basis calculation giving faithful flatness, rather than inferring it
   from freeness without checking rank. For any $A$-module $N$ define $T\otimes_A
   N\longrightarrow\bigoplus_{i\in I}N$ by $t\otimes n\longmapsto(c_i(t)n)_i$, where $c_i(t)$
   are the finitely supported basis coordinates of $t$. Its inverse sends a finitely supported
   family $(n_i)_i$ to $\sum_i e_i\otimes n_i$. The tensor universal property, bilinearity, and
   $t=\sum_i c_i(t)e_i$ check both composites on pure tensors and basis-coordinate families; the
   maps commute with every map of $N$. Thus tensoring any complex with $T$ is the direct sum of
   its coordinate copies. A direct sum of an exact complex is exact: lift each of the finitely
   many nonzero coordinates of a kernel element. Conversely, if that direct-sum complex is
   exact, fix $i_0\in I$, insert a kernel element in coordinate $i_0$, lift in the direct sum,
   and project back to $i_0$ to find its preimage. Hence the complex is exact if and only if its
   tensor with $T$ is exact, which is precisely faithful flatness of $A\to T$. This argument
   covers infinite $I$ as well as finite nonzero rank.
   Uses: def-weil-pbc-free-module, def-weil-pbc-module-tensor-product, def-weil-pbc-faithfully-
   flat-ring-map.

6. Apply regular-ring faithfully flat descent to $A\to T$. Step 5 supplies faithful flatness and
   step 4 supplies regularity of $T$, including its Noetherianity. Its conclusion is that
   $A=R_a$ is a regular ring. This uses the descent theorem with exactly its stated hypotheses,
   without assuming that $R$ was already regular.
   Uses: lem-weil-pbc-regular-ring-faithfully-flat-descent.

7. Let $U=D(a)\subseteq\operatorname{Spec}R$. It is open by the basic-open lemma and nonempty
   because the prime $(0)$ of the nonzero domain $R$ avoids $a\ne0$. For every $\mathfrak p\in
   U$ the same lemma supplies the corresponding prime $P$ of $A=R_a$. As in step 2,
   $(R_a)_P\cong R_{\mathfrak p}$ by $(r/a^m)/(s/a^n)\mapsto ra^n/(sa^m)$ with inverse
   $r/s\mapsto(r/1)/(s/1)$. The nonvanishing of the denominators modulo $\mathfrak p$ and the
   fraction relations verify these formulas. Since $A$ is regular by step 6, its prime
   localization at $P$, and therefore $R_{\mathfrak p}$, is regular local. This is the required
   nonempty regular open in $\operatorname{Spec}R$.
   Uses: lem-weil-pbc-spec-localization-away-basic-open, def-weil-ring-localization-away, def-
   weil-pbc-localization-at-prime, def-weil-regular-scheme. -/
theorem exists_nonempty_regular_open_of_finiteType
    (R : Type u) (S : Type v) [CommRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R] [IsDomain R]
    [CommRing S] [IsDomain S] (f : R →+* S)
    (hinj : Function.Injective f) (hft : f.FiniteType)
    (hS : ∃ V : Set (PrimeSpectrum S), IsOpen V ∧ V.Nonempty ∧
      ∀ q ∈ V, IsRegularLocalRing (Localization.AtPrime q.asIdeal)) :
    ∃ U : Set (PrimeSpectrum R), IsOpen U ∧ U.Nonempty ∧
      ∀ p ∈ U, IsRegularLocalRing (Localization.AtPrime p.asIdeal) := by
  sorry

end RingHom
